menu "Detector Wi-Fi dashboard"

    config FY_DASHBOARD_SSID_PREFIX
        string "Access point name prefix"
        default "FY-"
        help
            The access point is named this prefix followed by four random
            characters chosen on first use, for example FY-K7QM. Avoid names
            that announce what the device is.

    config FY_DASHBOARD_PASSKEY
        string "Shared access point passkey (empty: one per device)"
        default ""
        help
            Empty: each device generates its own temporary passkey on first
            use and shows it on its display. Boards without a display (the
            XIAO) use this shared passkey instead; it must be 8-63
            characters. Anyone who knows a shared passkey can join any
            device built with it while that device's dashboard is on.

    config FY_DASHBOARD_CHANNEL
        int "Access point channel"
        default 6
        range 1 13

    config FY_DASHBOARD_IDLE_S
        int "End a dashboard session after this many seconds without use"
        default 600
        range 60 3600
        help
            A session ends when nobody has pressed the button or loaded the
            status page for this long. Detection then resumes in the selected
            mode. The page does not refresh by itself, so an open browser
            tab does not keep the session alive.

    config FY_DASHBOARD_CAPTIVE_PORTAL
        bool "Test only: present the dashboards as a captive portal"
        default n
        help
            For evaluating phone behaviour; the design plan does not adopt a
            captive portal. The dashboard networks' DHCP servers also offer
            the device as gateway and DNS server, a DNS server answers every
            name with the device's address, and the HTTP server redirects
            everything except the status page there, including operating
            systems' connectivity checks. Phones then open the page in their
            "sign in to network" window with mobile data still on. On a PC
            the USB link also carries a default route (normally outranked by
            faster links). Built by the *_portal environments.

endmenu
